# Neo Ensemble parameters (docs/specs/machines.md §5). The order is permanent: songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoEnsemble
namespace NeoBuzz.Ensemble
class NeoEnsembleMachine
summary Chorus, flanger and phaser in one: one to four modulated delays (Chorus), a short swept delay with feedback (Flanger) or 2–12 swept allpass stages (Phaser), with an LFO in Hz or synced to the tempo, stereo spread, a tone control in the wet path and feedback of −99 … +99 % through a saturating loop that can't run away. Zero latency; at Mix 0 % it passes the input bit for bit. Use it to thicken, widen and move a sound: chorus on pads, keys and guitars, a flanger's jet on drums and leads, a phaser's sweep on keys and pads.

group Mode
Mode      "Mode"      0 2    0    enum Chorus Flanger Phaser          | Chorus: 1–4 voices of a modulated delay; Flanger: one short swept delay with feedback (a comb); Phaser: allpass stages swept in frequency
    how Chooses the effect: a multi-voice chorus, a swept flanger or a phaser.
    sound Chorus thickens and widens, the flanger sweeps a jet-like comb through the sound, and the phaser adds a softer, vowel-like swirl.
group LFO
Rate      "Rate"      0 1000 492  freq 0.02 20                        | LFO speed, when Sync is Free
    how Sets the speed of the sweep from 0.02 to 20 Hz when Sync is Free.
    sound Slow rates give a gentle drift and fast rates a vibrato-like wobble.
Sync      "Sync"      0 13   0    enum Free 1/32 1/16T 1/16 1/8T 1/8 1/4T 1/4 1/2 1_bar 2_bars 4_bars 8_bars 16_bars | Tempo sync: one LFO cycle per division of the song's tempo (overrides Rate)
    how Locks one sweep cycle to a note length of the song tempo instead of the Rate knob.
    sound Synced sweeps breathe in time with the track.
Shape     "Shape"     0 1    0    enum Sine Triangle                  | LFO waveform
    how Chooses a sine or a triangle for the sweep.
    sound Sine is smooth and even; triangle moves at a steady speed and sounds slightly more mechanical.
Depth     "Depth"     0 1000 300  pct                                 | Sweep: at 100 % the delay moves an octave either side of Delay (Chorus), two octaves (Flanger); the phaser's frequency three octaves either side of Centre
    how Sets how far the sweep moves the delay or the filter frequency, from 0 to 100 %.
    sound Low depth is a subtle shimmer, and high depth is a dramatic, obvious sweep.
Spread    "Spread"    0 180  90   lin 0 180 ° 0                       | The right channel's LFO phase against the left's: 0° moves both alike, 180° opposite (with several chorus voices, a share of the gap between them: at 180° the right's voices fall halfway between the left's)
    how Offsets the right channel's sweep against the left, from 0 to 180 degrees.
    sound At 0 degrees both sides move together; wider values make the movement circle through the stereo picture.
group Sound
Delay     "Delay"     0 1000 710  time 0.0002 0.03                    | Chorus and Flanger: the delay at the centre of the sweep (geometric), 0.2–30 ms
    how Sets the delay at the centre of the sweep for Chorus and Flanger, from 0.2 to 30 ms.
    sound Short delays make a metallic flanger tone, and longer ones a thicker, more detuned chorus.
Voices    "Voices"    1 4    2    int 0                               | Chorus: delayed voices, their LFOs spread evenly over the cycle
    how Sets the number of chorus voices, from 1 to 4.
    sound More voices make a lusher, denser ensemble; one voice is a clear single doubling.
Centre    "Centre"    0 1000 469  freq 50 10000                       | Phaser: the stages' frequency at the centre of the sweep (geometric)
    how Sets the middle frequency of the phaser sweep, from 50 Hz to 10 kHz.
    sound A low centre swirls the body of the sound and a high one makes the sweep thinner and airier.
Stages    "Stages"    2 12   4    int 0                               | Phaser: first-order allpass stages (an even count gives Stages ÷ 2 notches)
    how Sets the number of phaser stages, from 2 to 12.
    sound More stages add more notches and a deeper, more complex swirl.
Feedback  "Feedback"  0 1980 990  lin -99 99 % 1                      | The wet signal back into the delay or the stages; negative inverts it. Bounded to ±99 % and saturating above full scale, so it can't run away
    how Feeds the wet signal back into the effect, from minus 99 % to plus 99 %.
    sound Positive feedback sharpens the peaks into a ringing resonance, and negative feedback gives a hollower tone.
Tone      "Tone"      0 1000 862  freq 500 20000                      | One-pole high cut, −3 dB at this frequency, on the wet signal and in the feedback loop (Chorus, Flanger) or in the loop (Phaser); at its top (20 kHz) it is open
    how Rolls off the highs of the wet signal from 500 Hz to 20 kHz.
    sound Lowering it makes the effect darker and softer, and fully open it stays bright.
group Output
Mix       "Mix"       0 1000 500  pct                                 | Dry against wet: 50 % the deepest comb or notches, 100 % only the wet (vibrato)
    how Blends the dry sound with the effect from 0 to 100 %.
    sound Around 50 % gives the deepest notches, while 100 % leaves only the pitch-wobbling wet sound.
Output    "Output"    0 240  120  db -12 12                           | Output gain, −12 … +12 dB in 0.1 dB steps
    how Sets the output gain, from minus 12 to plus 12 dB.
    sound Use it to match the level with the dry sound.
